When the user quits, Ladybird asks every window’s pages whether they
may unload, one window at a time, cancels the whole quit if any page
refuses, and only once all of them agree asks about active downloads
and closes the windows. Chromium’s BrowserCloseManager does the same:
TryToCloseBrowsers() runs each browser’s beforeunload handlers through
UnloadController::TryToCloseWindow(), CancelBrowserClose() cancels the
quit if one of them is refused, CheckForDownloadsInProgress() follows,
and CloseBrowsers() shuts down the session services before closing
anything, so an exit can restore every window that was open. Firefox’s
CanCloseWindow() likewise asks every tab’s permitUnload() before a
window closes for a quit.
